2026-08-31T14:54Z claude note upstream v1.12.0 (2026-08-31) moved two of the caps this ticket is about. #38 corrected the full-text cap's own documentation: a blank value keeps meaning the default 40000 whether or not full-text indexing is on, and 0 means no cap but will not render in Claude Desktop's number input, which made the setting look rejected. #37 added a hard refusal of the whole full-text pass under Electron, and #39 added ZOTEUS_INDEX_FULLTEXT_CONCURRENCY plus a degradation back-off. So which cap binds where now depends on the host and the serving API, not on the setting alone. Re-read against v1.12.0
2026-09-03T07:59Z claude note THE CAPS DO NOT REACH THE SDT PATH, and upstream already drains the truncated half. Read 2026-09-03 from zotero/zotero MAIN over raw.githubusercontent.com -- NOT the installed build 20260817151751, which the session container cannot reach; artifact and scope caveat at verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md, and the one-line check that closes the gap is a grep of the extracted omni.ja on doudou for reindexTruncated. (1) sdt.js has ZERO occurrences of Prefs, maxPages, maxLength, limit, truncat or cap -- an empty grep, not a sparse one -- and generates through Zotero.PDFWorker.getStructuredDocumentText(item.id, {isPriority, onProgress}). In pdfWorker/manager.js the two entry points differ exactly there: getFullText(itemID, maxPages, isPriority, password) at line 612 forwards maxPages into the worker query at 629, which fulltext.js:651 supplies as allPages ? null : maxPages, while getStructuredDocumentText at 652 sends {buf, contentType, password, sourceHash, reportProgress} at 677 with NO page or length argument, the whole file buffer going over; manager.js reads no preference anywhere. NOT ESTABLISHED: a limit internal to the WASM document-worker would appear in none of these three files. The cheap empirical control is the IPCC volume 65F79PTJ, 2 913 pages and already complete flat: open it in the reader, read the pack page catalog with verification/probes/sdt_read.py, and 2 913 against 100 settles it. The two packs on the machine are 26 and 24 pages, far too short to test it. (2) THE FINDING LARGER THAN THE QUESTION: fulltext.js:345-362 registers preference observers on fulltext.textMaxLength and fulltext.pdfMaxPages, each scheduling, debounced 5 s on the settled value, this.reindexTruncated(type, limit) at line 3093, whose whole body selects itemID from fulltextItems where indexedPages IS NOT NULL and indexedPages < totalPages and indexedPages < ? (and the indexedChars/totalChars twin) and then calls indexItems with ignoreErrors. Its own comment gives the intent: re-extract just the items it affects rather than forcing a full rebuild, which would re-upload everything. So UPSTREAM ALREADY HOLDS BOTH HALVES OF THIS TICKET -- the truncation ledger is fulltextItems.indexedPages/totalPages and indexedChars/totalChars, and the drain is reindexTruncated triggered by the preference itself -- and it pays the same treadmill this project has been reasoning about, bounded the same way, by the ledger rather than by a verb. Action 3's honest state should be read against that rather than designed from scratch. (3) THE DECOMPOSITION THIS FORCES, and only one half is ours: a body with indexedPages < totalPages is drained by upstream on a cap change, IF the installed build carries this machinery; an OLD-GENERATION cache is never selected by that query at all, because a cache fully extracted under the old extractor has indexedPages == totalPages and is not truncated. So the 3 882 pre-form-feed PDF caches stay ours whatever the dating says, and the truncated half may not be. This narrows what our own extraction is for, and it should be settled before 0120's recommendation is written.
2026-09-03T08:37Z claude note TWO CORRECTIONS to the entry above, both from the Sonnet reviewer's REROLL on PR 256 (comment 5522963522), both its findings and not mine. (i) THE GREP CLAIM WAS OVERSTATED: the entry says sdt.js has ZERO occurrences of Prefs, maxPages, maxLength, limit, truncat or cap, an empty grep and not a sparse one. Five of those six are exactly that, case-insensitively. The sixth is not: the pattern actually run carried cap\b, which is empty, but BARE cap is not -- grep -ic cap returns 1, the word captured in the comment at sdt.js:287. Irrelevant to the conclusion, which stands unchanged, and the sentence was false as written. verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md now states the patterns actually run and names the one substring hit. (ii) Unrelated to this ticket but from the same review and recorded where the reader of these findings will be: the two same-day corrections to the DECISIONS.md entry were made by EDITING the entry in place rather than by appending, which the ledger's own header and AGENTS.md forbid and which the ledger's own precedent (DECISIONS.md 2051, 2193) shows the shape of -- a reversal names what it reverses and leaves it standing. The original entry is restored verbatim from 4f1d699 and the two corrections are now separate appended entries. The narrow reading that the rule names only RATIFIED entries and this one is awaiting ratification is not relied on: the harm the rule prevents is that the withdrawn text stops being readable in the file, and that harm was real here.
2026-09-03T09:31Z claude note Both open checks in verification/SDT-CAPS-0483.md ran on doudou, build 20260824184709 (not the 20260817151751 the note names). reindexTruncated IS in the installed omni.ja, twice, both in fulltext.js: the pref observer and the definition, no third call site, so no startup sweep. The WASM worker does NOT cap pages: 25 002 blocks over 2 913 catalog pages, 2 913 distinct pages carrying blocks, zero empty, no discontinuity at page 100. Stock defaults from the shipped code: pdfMaxPages 100, textMaxLength 500000. Action 1 answered by a natural experiment already on disk: the same byte-identical PDF is 2913/2913 in library 3 and 100/2913 in library 1. Third finding: 1 053 rows still have indexedPages<totalPages, all at exactly 100, while pdfMaxPages reads 999999, so the upstream drain being event-triggered means it never fired here.
--- body ---
## Context
The author raised it during the 0140 session (2026-08-30): Zotero as the
extract process carries caps we do not control — `fulltext.pdfMaxPages`
(default 100 pages) and `fulltext.textMaxLength` (default 500 000
characters), both at defaults on this machine (prefs.js holds no override).
The spec chain records neither. A long document therefore arrives truncated
at the source, before the 0140 chunk geometry or any coverage machinery
sees it — the same silent-partial failure family the truncation regression
guards at the embed stage, one stage earlier.

Measured state (2026-08-30, census data
`bench/results/0140-passage-census/census.json` and its per-file JSONL):
the regime is MIXED, so the mechanism is an open question, not a verdict.
152 of 8 700 PDF caches exceed ~90k tokens (≈100 dense pages); the tail
reaches 306k, 488k, and one 10,1M-token cache. Yet the p99 sits at 103 429
tokens, almost exactly the 500 000-character cap's signature (~105k
tokens). Consistent with: one cap binding the API-served indexed content,
the cache files partially escaping, historical extractions under other
settings — not yet isolated. Which artifact each cap binds matters because
zoteus consumes the API while our census read cache files directly.

## Relevant files
- `bench/results/0140-passage-census/census.json` — the measured cache-side state
- `SPEC.md` §4 — where the platform cap belongs once settled (beside C2)
- `SPEC.md` §5.2.3-5.2.4 — coverage states (cache-lost machinery) the
  truncated-body flag would join
- `tickets/0480-old-extractor-fulltext-quality-staleness.erg` — sibling
  extractor-quality class

## Actions
1. The one experiment: pick one known 500+-page PDF; compare the API's
   `indexedPages`/`totalPages` and content length against the cache file's
   length. That settles which cap binds which artifact.
2. Record the settled constraint in SPEC.md §4 (a ruling in
   DECISIONS.md first, per the chain's rule).
3. Design the honest state: the API reports `indexedPages`/`totalPages`, so
   an extractor-truncated body can be a marked ledger state (like
   cache-lost) instead of silence — one field, and R4/R17 stay true for
   books.

## Test
- The experiment of action 1 is the test; its artifact goes under
  `verification/` and is cited here.

## Verification
- [ ] Which cap binds the cache file, and which binds the API content, each
      established by the experiment, not inferred
- [ ] The constraint stated in the chain with the cap values and their scope
- [ ] The truncated-body state designed, with its detection source named

## Invariants
- No extractor improvement in this ticket — the author deferred that
  (2026-08-30); this ticket records, settles, and designs the honest flag.
- Coverage claims (R4, R17) must not silently include truncated bodies once
  the state exists.

## Exit criteria
- [ ] The cap mechanism is settled by measurement and recorded in the chain
- [ ] The truncated-body coverage state is in SPEC.md §5.2.4
- [ ] This ticket's findings are reflected in the extract-stage discussion
      whenever the own-extractor question reopens
